  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 122 PM CDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nThrough Wednesday: Latest RAP/water vapor analysis still showed \nan upper low situated over the southeast and a 595DM mid level \nridge over the Desert Southwest, leading to mean northerly dry \nflow across the Gulf Coast states. This will lock in PWs generally\naround one inch and low humidity aloft (i.e. 700-300mb layer \nhumidity in the low teens to single digits) and efficient mixing. \nThis will lead to moderating temperatures: highs becoming \nseasonably warm Wednesday (91F to 96F) and seasonable lows (71F to\n74F) Wednesday night. With afternoon mixing being the norm, heat \nstress will remain lower over most of the CWA but with a gradual \nuptick of heat index in southwestern areas (heat indices in the \n102F to 105F range by Wednesday afternoon). Thus, wl mention a \n\"Limited\" threat of heat stress in our southwest for Wednesday \nafternoon. Local radars this afternoon showed a few isolated light\nshowers close to our southwest indicative of a moisture increase.\nThe increase in low level moisture will bring low chances of \nisolated showers and storms to our southern most zones Wednesday \nafternoon as well. /22/DC/\n\nLate week into early next week (Thursday through next Monday): As \nthe upper low weakens the grip over the Southeast into Thursday, low \nlevel southerly return flow and an uptick in moisture (low and deep \nlayer moisture) will favor less efficient mixing, warmth/moist \nadvection and increasing heat stress late week through the weekend. \nTemperatures will be seasonably warm (highs in the 92F to 96F range \nand lows in the 72F to 76F range). The region will remain in more\nof a large scale weakness in the flow but low level flow will \nhelp moderate in higher surface dewpoints (70F to 76F) and \nincreasing heat stress concerns.\n\nEven though large scale pattern is somewhat similar to what we have \nbeen observing, the key difference is the low level return moist \nadvection and increased deepening moisture aloft (PWs in the 1.5 to \n2 inch range by the weekend into early next week). Rain chances will \nmainly be summertime variety (diurnally induced) with isolated to at \ntimes scattered coverage (20 to 40 percent range). This pattern \nwill not favor any noteworthy storm threat, but a few scattered \nstronger gusty storms with locally heavy rain cannot be ruled out,\nespecially later into the weekend.\n\nThe primary hazardous weather concern remains increasing heat stress \ninto next week. By Thursday, the area of \"Limited\" risk where \ntemperatures are expected to reach to near 105 F expands to include \nthe entire area into next week while an \"Elevated\" risk area is \nintroduced that highlights a risk for heat indices above 105 F for \nthe southwestern two-thirds of the area. This period of heat danger \nwill generally become more entrenched as we move into and through \nthe weekend and upgrades to \"Significant\" risk areas as well as heat \nadvisories/warnings could be necessary by later this week. /86/DC/",
